**Merit**

If the fields are final, we cannot assign a value to them afterwards, but we can assign values to final fields in the copy constructor. Adding a field to a class is also simple, since only the copy constructor needs to be changed.

**Demerit**

A copy constructor is not inherited by the child class. If a parent class is used to refer to a child class, the child's copy constructor cannot be used.

```cpp
class distance
{
    int feet;
    int inches;
public:
    distance(int x1 = 0, int x2 = 0)
    {
        feet = x1;
        inches = x2;
    }
};
```

An **inline function** is a function that is expanded in line when it is invoked, i.e. the compiler replaces the function call with the function definition.

```cpp
inline float square(float x)
{
    return x * x;
}

int main()
{
    float a, y;
    a = square(y);     // becomes a = y * y;
}
```

**When inline expansion does not work:**

- For functions returning values, if a loop, a switch or a goto exists.
- For functions not returning values, if a return statement exists.
- If the function contains static variables.
- If the inline function is recursive.

Note: member functions defined inside a class are inline by default.

**Operator overloading** is a compile-time polymorphism in which an operator is overloaded to give it a special meaning for a user-defined data type. It can be used to redefine most of the operators in C++.

```cpp
class matrix {
    int a[3][3];
public:
    void operator+(matrix x)
    {
        int mat[3][3];
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
        {
            for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++)
            {
                mat[i][j] = a[i][j] + x.a[i][j];   // addition
            }
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
        {
            cout << "_ _";
            for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++)
            {
                cout << mat[i][j] << "\t";
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};
```

**Operator overloading of `+` for complex numbers**

The `+` operator can be overloaded either as a member function (left operand is the invoking object, so it takes one explicit argument) or as a friend function (non-member, takes both operands as arguments).

#### (i) Using a friend function

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Complex {
    float re, im;
public:
    Complex(float r = 0, float i = 0) : re(r), im(i) {}
    friend Complex operator+(Complex a, Complex b);   // friend declaration
    void show() const { cout << re << " + " << im << "i" << endl; }
};

Complex operator+(Complex a, Complex b) {             // not a member
    return Complex(a.re + b.re, a.im + b.im);
}

int main() {
    Complex c1(2, 3), c2(4, 5), c3;
    c3 = c1 + c2;          // operator+(c1, c2)
    c3.show();             // 6 + 8i
}
```

#### (ii) Using a member function

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Complex {
    float re, im;
public:
    Complex(float r = 0, float i = 0) : re(r), im(i) {}
    Complex operator+(const Complex &b) const {       // member: one argument
        return Complex(re + b.re, im + b.im);
    }
    void show() const { cout << re << " + " << im << "i" << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Complex c1(2, 3), c2(4, 5), c3;
    c3 = c1 + c2;          // c1.operator+(c2)
    c3.show();             // 6 + 8i
}
```

#### Different syntaxes of using `+`

| Syntax | Meaning (member version) | Meaning (friend version) |
|---|---|---|
| `c3 = c1 + c2;` | `c3 = c1.operator+(c2);` | `c3 = operator+(c1, c2);` |
| `c3 = c1.operator+(c2);` | explicit call, allowed | not allowed (not a member) |
| `c3 = operator+(c1, c2);` | not valid | explicit call, allowed |
| `c3 = c1 + 5;` | works if constructor `Complex(float)` exists (5 converts to Complex) | works |
| `c3 = 5 + c1;` | **error** (left operand is not an object, cannot call member) | works (implicit conversion on left operand) |

Key points:
- Member `+` needs the left operand to be an object of the class; the friend version allows implicit conversion on both operands.
- Binary operator as member takes 1 argument, as friend takes 2 arguments.
- Precedence, associativity and number of operands of `+` cannot be changed by overloading.

Class `STRING` with a dynamically allocated character array, a copy constructor (deep copy), `operator+` for concatenation and `operator<<` to display the results.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>
using namespace std;

class STRING {
    char *str;
public:
    STRING(const char *s = "") {                 // parameterised / default constructor
        str = new char[strlen(s) + 1];
        strcpy(str, s);
    }
    STRING(const STRING &o) {                    // copy constructor (deep copy)
        str = new char[strlen(o.str) + 1];
        strcpy(str, o.str);
    }
    STRING operator+(const STRING &o) const {    // concatenation
        char *t = new char[strlen(str) + strlen(o.str) + 1];
        strcpy(t, str);
        strcat(t, o.str);
        STRING temp(t);
        delete[] t;
        return temp;                             // copy constructor used here
    }
    friend ostream &operator<<(ostream &out, const STRING &s) {
        out << s.str;
        return out;
    }
    ~STRING() { delete[] str; }
};

int main() {
    STRING s1 = "HELLO";
    cout << "s1 = " << s1 << endl;
    STRING s2 = "WORLD";
    cout << "s2 = " << s2 << endl;
    STRING s3 = s1 + s2;                         // copy constructor initialises s3
    cout << "s3 = " << s3 << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
s1 = HELLO
s2 = WORLD
s3 = HELLOWORLD
```

The copy constructor performs a deep copy so that `s3` owns its own memory and destruction of the temporary does not leave dangling pointers.

