An object created locally inside a function can be returned **by value** to the calling function; the compiler copies it (using the copy constructor, or elides the copy) so it remains valid after the local one is destroyed. (Returning a reference/pointer to a local object would be wrong, since it dies on return; to return a pointer, create the object with `new`.)

