A **function template** defines a generic function that works for any data type; the compiler generates the type-specific version when it is called. A template function can call itself, so recursion works exactly as with an ordinary function, with the type parameter carried through each recursive call. Each recursive call uses the same instantiation `T`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// recursive template: sum of first n elements of an array of any type
template <class T>
T arraySum(T arr[], int n) {
    if (n <= 0)                       // base case
        return 0;
    return arr[n - 1] + arraySum(arr, n - 1);   // recursive call
}

// recursive template: power x^n
template <class T>
T power(T x, int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 1;
    return x * power(x, n - 1);
}

int main() {
    int   a[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    float b[] = {1.5f, 2.5f, 3.0f};
    cout << arraySum(a, 5) << endl;      // 15   (T = int)
    cout << arraySum(b, 3) << endl;      // 7    (T = float)
    cout << power(2, 5) << endl;         // 32
    cout << power(1.5, 2) << endl;       // 2.25
}
```

Points:
- Base case is required to stop recursion, as usual.
- For `arraySum(a,5)` the compiler creates `int arraySum(int[], int)`, which recursively calls the same instantiation.
- Templates give one definition for many types, avoiding code duplication.

